  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Saturday)\nIssued at 1140 AM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nLingering showers and a few thunderstorms will be possible mainly\nalong and north of I-44 through the mid morning hours Sunday as\nthe front slowly tracks into northeastern Oklahoma. Additional\nthunderstorm development is likely along the actual frontal\nboundary by late Sunday morning into the afternoon. Initial\ndevelopment is expected across northeast Oklahoma, eventually\ngrowing into one or more southeastward advancing clusters through\nSunday evening. Initial cells could be strong to severe, posing a\ndamaging wind and hail threat through the afternoon hours. As \nclusters become more organized, a more widespread damaging wind \nthreat could develop into the evening, along with locally heavy \nrainfall. Most of this activity should exit the forecast area\novernight Sunday. Scattered showers or storms could linger into\nMonday morning within the elevated frontal zone/WAA regime that \nwill stall out over eastern Oklahoma.\n\nA much more seasonal airmass will arrive in the wake of the front\nand finally put an end to the prolonged period of extreme heat.\nGenerally near normal temperatures are expected through much of\nnext week, with low humidity and light winds through the period.\nTemperatures could even trend somewhat below normal on Monday and\nTuesday as lingering cloud cover remains over the region through\nmuch of the day. The forecast will remain mostly dry through the \nrest of the period after Monday, as mid level ridging remains\nin the vicinity and builds more over the Southern Plains by late \nweek. A few periods of showers may try to sneak into northeast\nOklahoma through the week, but otherwise a dry forecast will\nprevail into the latter part of the week when another front could\napproach the region.\n\nBowlan",
